Yes. The architecture gets much more interesting once you realize the canonical sport does not need to be the rendered video.

You could have a persistent simulated league where the actual “game” is a structured world-state:

S_t =
\{\text{players, ball, physics, tactics, fatigue, injuries, psychology, rules, weather, coaching}\}

Each athlete is an agent with a continually updated model—speed curves, decision tendencies, route preferences, reaction latency, risk tolerance, fatigue behavior, chemistry with teammates, adaptation to opponents, etc. Current systems already demonstrate pieces of this: Google Research Football supports multi-agent play inside a physics-based 3D football simulator, including running without GPU rendering, specifically separating simulation from visualization.  EA has separately used real match/volumetric data and machine learning to reproduce player-specific movement and behavioral characteristics in its football games. 

Then you split the stack:

\boxed{
\text{World simulation}
\rightarrow
\text{canonical event stream}
\rightarrow
\text{camera/director}
\rightarrow
\text{renderer}
\rightarrow
\text{viewer}
}

That separation is huge.

The simulator might calculate thousands of possible futures faster than real time. You don’t need to render them. Rendering is expensive because humans want pixels; the agents mostly need state variables. Google’s older football environment was already designed to run extremely quickly with rendering disabled—roughly 25 million simulation steps per day on a six-core machine in its reported setup. 

So you could run enormous amounts of off-screen experience:

training camp → millions of reps
QB vs. coverage → millions of encounters
coach-agent experimentation → thousands of playbooks
rookie development → simulated practice histories
scouting → counterfactual matchups
injury/load models → alternative usage patterns

Then when Sunday arrives, you slow the canonical timeline to human viewing speed.

And the broadcast itself becomes programmable. Same canonical play, but I could watch:

Traditional broadcast — familiar NFL-style direction.
QB view — dynamically framed around what the quarterback could perceive.
All-22++ — tactical overlays showing developing concepts.
Cinematic — AI director predicts where the decisive interaction will happen and positions the virtual camera perfectly.
My broadcast — follows the fantasy players I care about and emphasizes their matchups.
Replay counterfactual — “show what probably happens if the safety bites on the underneath route.”

Because there is no physical camera, you can put it anywhere that improves comprehension. You could even reconstruct an impossible view—something between Madden, SkyCam, All-22 and film study—that eventually feels more natural than today’s television angle.

Then your league idea goes beyond individual games.

Each agent persists:

\text{rookie}
\rightarrow
\text{development}
\rightarrow
\text{contracts}
\rightarrow
\text{trades}
\rightarrow
\text{injuries}
\rightarrow
\text{adaptation}
\rightarrow
\text{aging}
\rightarrow
\text{retirement}

Now you’ve created actual sports history rather than isolated simulations.

Teams could develop cultures. Coaches could spawn coaching trees. Players could learn tendencies. Draft classes could enter annually. Franchises could rebuild. Rules could evolve. The league could maintain records for 100 simulated seasons.

Then economies naturally attach to it: franchises, salaries, free agency, draft capital, fantasy leagues, sponsorships, collectibles, prediction markets where legally permitted, media rights, merchandise, user-owned expansion teams, scouting businesses, analytics services, even an entire sports journalism layer covering events that happened only inside the simulation.

There is one important correction I’d make to “agents coded exactly like the players”: exactly is probably neither technically attainable nor necessary.

Better:

\text{real player data}
\rightarrow
\text{behavioral model}
\rightarrow
\text{uncertainty distribution}

You don’t claim “this is Patrick Mahomes.” You model observable tendencies with uncertainty and continuously recalibrate against reality. If you wanted actual NFL identities, likenesses, team marks, etc., licensing/publicity/IP agreements become a major business constraint. A fictional league inspired by real athletic distributions avoids much of that problem.

And then there’s an even stranger version.

Eventually you stop copying humans.

Let the league’s agents train beyond human precedent while retaining football’s physical constraints. Novel quarterbacks, defensive structures, route concepts and player archetypes emerge that no coach explicitly programmed.

Then you have:

\text{simulation of human football}
\rightarrow
\text{AI football}
\rightarrow
\text{a new sport descended from football}

And because the simulation can learn faster than you can render it, the audience might literally be watching a civilization of sport whose internal history is advancing vastly faster than the hours we spend watching it.

That part of your idea is the real unlock: rendering becomes the peephole, not the world.